window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert11-bio-living-world","kind":"practice","exam":"NEET","year":null,"title":"The Living World · Practice","chapter":"The Living World","subject":"Biology","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-001","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":1,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following taxa are correctly matched with their taxonomic rank, as per NCERT's classification of common organisms?\nPrimata – Order\nInsecta – Class\nSapindales – Family\nPoaceae – Family\nAngiospermae – Class\nMuscidae – Family","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Primata is the order of man, so it is correct.\nInsecta is the class of the housefly, so it is correct.\nSapindales is the order of mango, not a family, so it is incorrect.\nPoaceae is the family of wheat, so it is correct.\nAngiospermae is a division, not a class, so it is incorrect.\nMuscidae is the family of the housefly, so it is correct.\nFour pairs are correctly matched.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-002","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":2,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Naming an organism is possible only after the organism has been identified.\nReason (R): Identification means describing the organism correctly, so that we know which organism the name is attached to.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false.","A is false but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that nomenclature is only possible when the organism is described correctly.\nR is true. Identification is defined as describing the organism correctly so we know which organism the name refers to.\nR directly explains A. A name cannot be attached reliably until we know exactly which organism is being named.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-003","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":3,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"In the classification of plants, families are characterised on the basis of:","image":"","options":["Vegetative features only","Reproductive features only","Both vegetative and reproductive features","Habitat and economic uses only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that families are characterised on the basis of both vegetative and reproductive features of plant species.\nUsing only one type of feature would be incomplete.\nClassification based on 'uses' was the earliest, human-centred approach. It is not how a family is defined.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-004","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":4,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Cats and dogs share some similarities but differ in others. They are placed in different families but in the same order. Which order is this?","image":"","options":["Primata","Carnivora","Polymoniales","Diptera"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Cats belong to family Felidae and dogs belong to family Canidae.\nThe animal order Carnivora contains both Felidae and Canidae.\nPrimata is the order of monkeys, gorillas and gibbons.\nPolymoniales is a plant order, and Diptera is the order of the housefly.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-005","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":5,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about taxonomic categories are correct?\n(i) Classification is a single-step process.\n(ii) All taxonomic categories together make up the taxonomic hierarchy.\n(iii) Each category is a unit of classification and represents a rank.\n(iv) In plants, classes with a few similar characters are placed in a higher category called division.\n(v) Taxonomic groups are merely morphological aggregates, not distinct biological entities.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Incorrect: classification involves a hierarchy of steps, and each step is a rank or category.\n(ii) Correct: all the categories together constitute the taxonomic hierarchy.\n(iii) Correct: each category is a unit of classification, represents a rank and is termed a taxon.\n(iv) Correct: for plants, division is used in place of phylum.\n(v) Incorrect: NCERT says taxonomic groups are distinct biological entities, not merely morphological aggregates.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-006","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":6,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT according to NCERT?","image":"","options":["Taxonomic studies are useful in agriculture, forestry and industry, and in knowing our bio-resources.","Classification groups anything into convenient categories based on some easily observable characters.","Systematics, from its very beginning, was limited only to giving scientific names to organisms.","Principles for naming plants are provided in the ICBN, and those for animals in the ICZN."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the NCERT summary lists these uses of taxonomic studies.\n(2) Correct: this is NCERT's own definition of classification.\n(3) Incorrect: systematics originally dealt with the diversity of organisms and the relationships among them. Its scope was later enlarged to include identification, nomenclature and classification. It was never limited to naming.\n(4) Correct: ICBN covers plants and ICZN covers animals.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-007","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":7,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Biological names are written in italics only when the word is originally derived from Latin.\nStatement II: In a biological name, the generic name begins with a capital letter while the specific epithet begins with a small letter.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. Biological names are Latinised or derived from Latin whatever their origin, so every biological name is printed in italics.\nStatement II is correct. The genus begins with a capital letter and the specific epithet with a small letter, as in Mangifera indica.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-008","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":8,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is a defining characteristic of living organisms?","image":"","options":["Growth","Ability to make sound","Reproduction","Response to external stimuli"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: a defining property must be present in all living organisms and absent in non-living things. Cellular organisation, metabolism and consciousness qualify. Consciousness is the ability to sense the surroundings and respond to stimuli.\n(1) Growth: not defining. Non-living things such as crystals and sand mounds also increase in mass, by accumulation from outside.\n(2) Making sound: many organisms, such as plants and bacteria, make no sound.\n(3) Reproduction: tempting, but not defining. Mules, sterile worker bees and infertile humans are alive yet cannot reproduce.\n(4) Response to stimuli: every organism, from prokaryotes to plants and animals, senses and responds to its environment.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 1 (MCQ Q9)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-009","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":9,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Which option arranges the following categories in ascending order, from lower to higher rank?\nFamily, Class, Genus, Order","image":"","options":["Genus → Order → Family → Class","Family → Genus → Order → Class","Genus → Family → Order → Class","Class → Order → Family → Genus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The ascending hierarchy is species → genus → family → order → class → phylum/division → kingdom.\nSo the given four categories run Genus → Family → Order → Class.\nOption (4) gives the descending order, not the ascending one.\nOption (1) wrongly places order below family.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-010","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":10,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Taxonomists have developed sub-categories within the broad taxonomic hierarchy so that taxa can be placed more soundly and scientifically.\nStatement II: As we move up the hierarchy from species to kingdom, members of a taxon share more common characteristics, so relationships with other taxa at the same level become easier to determine.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Species to kingdom are broad categories, and NCERT notes that taxonomists added sub-categories to allow more sound and scientific placement of taxa.\nStatement II is incorrect on both counts.\nFirst, going from species to kingdom, the number of shared characteristics decreases; the lower the taxon, the more characters its members share.\nSecond, the higher the category, the greater the difficulty of determining relationships to other taxa at the same level, so classification becomes more complex.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-011","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":11,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"In which of the following pairs is the first taxon placed at a HIGHER level than the second?","image":"","options":["Wheat – Plants","Dogs – Animals","Mammals – Dogs","Rice – Plants"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A higher taxon includes the lower taxa within it.\nA dog is a mammal, so 'Mammals' is a higher-level taxon than 'Dogs'.\nIn the other pairs the order is reversed:\n- Wheat and rice are kinds of plants, so 'Plants' is the higher taxon in options (1) and (4).\n- Dogs are animals, so 'Animals' is the higher taxon in option (2).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-012","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":12,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"When a biological name such as Panthera leo is written by hand instead of being printed, the correct way to show its Latin origin is to:","image":"","options":["Write both words in capital letters","Underline both words separately","Put both words within quotation marks","Underline only the generic name"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT rule: both words of a biological name are separately underlined when handwritten. When printed, they are written in italics instead.\nUnderlining only the genus leaves the specific epithet unmarked, so the rule is not followed.\nCapital letters and quotation marks are not part of the rules.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-013","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":13,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Characterisation, identification, classification and nomenclature are the basic processes of:","image":"","options":["Taxonomy","Ecology","Physiology","Morphology"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT states that characterisation, identification, classification and nomenclature are the processes basic to taxonomy.\nTaxonomy classifies organisms into different taxa based on their characteristics.\nEcology, physiology and morphology are other branches of biology. They are not built on these four processes.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-014","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":14,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following taxa listed in NCERT Table 1.1 are at the rank of family?\nHominidae, Diptera, Anacardiaceae, Poales, Muscidae, Sapindales, Poaceae, Primata","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The family-rank taxa are:\n- Hominidae (man)\n- Muscidae (housefly)\n- Anacardiaceae (mango)\n- Poaceae (wheat)\nThe rest are orders: Diptera, Poales, Sapindales and Primata.\nSo the count of families is 4.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-015","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":15,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Nomenclature of an organism is possible only after its identification.\nReason (R): A name can be given only when the organism is described correctly and we know to which organism the name is attached.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT says naming is only possible when the organism has first been identified.\nR is true: identification means describing the organism correctly so that we know which organism the name refers to.\nR states exactly why naming depends on identification, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-016","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":16,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Using the taxonomic placement of man, housefly, mango and wheat given in NCERT, evaluate the following:\nStatement I: Mango and wheat belong to the same division but to different classes.\nStatement II: Man and housefly belong to the same phylum but to different classes.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Mango and wheat are both in the division Angiospermae. Mango is in the class Dicotyledonae and wheat in the class Monocotyledonae.\nStatement II is incorrect. Man and housefly do belong to different classes, Mammalia and Insecta. However, their phyla also differ: man is in Chordata and housefly in Arthropoda.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-017","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":17,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (parts of the name 'Mangifera indica Linn.' or related terms) with List-II (what they represent).\nList-I:\n(A) Mangifera\n(B) indica\n(C) Linn.\n(D) ICBN\nList-II:\n(I) Code giving principles for naming plants\n(II) Specific epithet\n(III) Abbreviated name of the author who first described the species\n(IV) Genus","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A: Mangifera is the first word, so it is the genus (IV).\nB: indica is the second word, the specific epithet (II).\nC: Linn. is the abbreviated author citation, showing that Linnaeus first described the species (III).\nD: ICBN provides the agreed principles for naming plants (I).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-018","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":18,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Read the two statements about the universal rules of biological nomenclature.\nStatement I: The first word of a biological name, denoting the genus, starts with a capital letter, while the specific epithet starts with a small letter.\nStatement II: When a biological name is handwritten, both words are underlined together by a single continuous line.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The generic name is capitalised and the specific epithet is in lower case, as in Mangifera indica.\nStatement II is incorrect. In handwriting, the two words are underlined separately, not with one continuous line, to show their Latin origin.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-019","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":19,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (organism) with List-II (family):\nList-I\nA. Man\nB. Housefly\nC. Mango\nD. Wheat\nList-II\n(i) Anacardiaceae\n(ii) Poaceae\n(iii) Hominidae\n(iv) Muscidae","image":"","options":["A-ii, B-i, C-iv, D-iii","A-iii, B-iv, C-i, D-ii","A-iii, B-iv, C-ii, D-i","A-iv, B-iii, C-i, D-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"These pairs come from Table 1.1 in NCERT:\nMan (Homo sapiens) belongs to Hominidae, so A-iii.\nHousefly (Musca domestica) belongs to Muscidae, so B-iv.\nMango (Mangifera indica) belongs to Anacardiaceae, so C-i.\nWheat (Triticum aestivum) belongs to Poaceae, so D-ii.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-020","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":20,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II regarding Ernst Mayr.\nList-I:\n(a) Balzan Prize\n(b) International Prize for Biology\n(c) Crafoord Prize\n(d) Joined Harvard's Faculty of Arts and Sciences\nList-II:\n(i) 1953\n(ii) 1999\n(iii) 1983\n(iv) 1994","image":"","options":["a-iii, b-iv, c-ii, d-i","a-iv, b-iii, c-ii, d-i","a-iii, b-ii, c-iv, d-i","a-i, b-iv, c-ii, d-iii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Balzan Prize: 1983, so (iii).\n(b) International Prize for Biology: 1994, so (iv).\n(c) Crafoord Prize: 1999, so (ii).\n(d) Mayr joined Harvard in 1953, so (i). He retired in 1975.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-021","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":21,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT Table 1.1, which organism belongs to the class Monocotyledonae?","image":"","options":["Mango","Housefly","Man","Wheat"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Wheat (Triticum aestivum) is classified as: family Poaceae, order Poales, class Monocotyledonae, division Angiospermae.\nMango is also in Angiospermae, but its class is Dicotyledonae. This makes mango the most tempting wrong option.\nMan belongs to class Mammalia and housefly to class Insecta.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-022","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":22,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"NCERT names four processes that are basic to taxonomy. Which of the following is NOT one of them?","image":"","options":["Characterisation","Domestication","Identification","Nomenclature"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT lists four processes basic to taxonomy: characterisation, identification, classification and nomenclature.\nDomestication means taming organisms for human use. It is not a taxonomic process.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-023","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":23,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"The system of naming organisms with two components, a generic name and a specific epithet, was given by:","image":"","options":["Ernst Mayr","Carolus Linnaeus","Charles Darwin","Alexander Agassiz"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that binomial nomenclature, naming with two components, was given by Carolus Linnaeus and is now used by biologists worldwide.\nErnst Mayr is credited with the biological species concept, not the binomial system.\nDarwin is linked with evolution, and Agassiz is only the name of Mayr's professorship.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-024","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":24,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about scientific names is correct?","image":"","options":["An organism found in two countries may legitimately carry two different scientific names","A scientific name already in use for a known organism may be reused for a newly discovered one","The description of an organism should enable people in any part of the world to arrive at the same name","ICZN provides the principles for naming flowering plants"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: scientific names ensure that each organism has only one name.\n(2) Incorrect: scientific names ensure that a name has not been used for any other known organism.\n(3) Correct: NCERT says the description should enable people anywhere in the world to arrive at the same name.\n(4) Incorrect: plant names follow ICBN; ICZN is the code for animals.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-025","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":25,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, classification is the process by which anything is grouped into convenient categories based on","image":"","options":["the local names used for organisms in different regions","some easily observable characters","the uses of organisms to human beings only","the geographical area in which organisms are found"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT defines classification as grouping anything into convenient categories based on some easily observable characters.\nFor example, we easily recognise groups such as plants, animals, dogs, cats or insects from features we can see.\nOption (3) is tempting because the earliest classifications were based on the uses of organisms. That was a historical basis, not the definition of classification.\nLocal names vary from place to place, so they cannot be the basis of classification. Habitat alone is also not the stated basis.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-026","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":26,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Panthera and Felis are two different genera.\n(ii) Solanum nigrum and Solanum tuberosum are different species of the same genus.\n(iii) In Homo sapiens, Homo is the specific epithet.\n(iv) Every genus must contain at least two species.\n(v) A species should be distinguishable from closely related species by distinct morphological differences.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: Panthera includes lion, tiger and leopard, while Felis includes cats.\n(ii) Correct: nigrum and tuberosum are two epithets under the same genus, Solanum.\n(iii) Incorrect: Homo is the genus and sapiens is the specific epithet.\n(iv) Incorrect: NCERT says a genus may have one or more than one specific epithet.\n(v) Correct: this is how NCERT says species are distinguished from one another.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-027","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":27,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Taxonomic key is one of the taxonomic tools in the identification and classification of plants and animals. It is used in the preparation of","image":"","options":["Monographs","Flora","Both a & b","None of these"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: a taxonomic key is based on contrasting characters (couplets). It is used to identify plants and animals.\nKeys are included in monographs, which deal with one taxon.\nKeys are also included in floras, which cover the plants of an area.\nIn both, readers use the key to identify the taxa described, so the tool serves both.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 1 (MCQ Q7)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-028","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":28,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"A cat and a dog show some similarities and some differences. To which families do they belong, respectively?","image":"","options":["Canidae and Felidae","Felidae and Hominidae","Felidae and Canidae","Muscidae and Canidae"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The differences between a cat and a dog are enough to place them in two separate families.\nThe cat (genus Felis) belongs to Felidae, and the dog belongs to Canidae.\nOption (1) names the correct families in the reverse order.\nHominidae is the family of man, and Muscidae is the family of the housefly.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-029","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":29,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"In the common taxonomic categories, which term is used for plants in place of 'phylum'?","image":"","options":["Class","Order","Division","Kingdom"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The common categories are kingdom, phylum or division (for plants), class, order, family, genus and species.\nIn plants, classes with a few similar characters are grouped into a Division.\nFor example, Angiospermae is the division for mango and wheat in Table 1.1.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-030","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":30,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the taxonomic category 'order' is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["An order is an assemblage of families that exhibit a few similar characters.","Order and higher categories are generally identified based on aggregates of characters.","The families within an order share more similar characters than the genera within a family do.","The order Carnivora includes the families Felidae and Canidae."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct. An order is an assemblage of families showing a few similar characters.\n(2) Correct. Order and higher categories are identified based on aggregates of characters.\n(3) Incorrect. The similar characters shared within an order are fewer than those shared by the genera within a family.\n(4) Correct. Carnivora includes both Felidae and Canidae.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-031","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":31,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The name 'Mangifera indica Linn.' indicates that this species was first described by Linnaeus.\nStatement II: In a biological name, the author's name is written in full between the generic name and the specific epithet.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The author citation 'Linn.' shows that Linnaeus first described this species.\nStatement II is incorrect on two counts.\nFirst, the author's name appears after the specific epithet, at the end of the name, not between genus and epithet.\nSecond, it is written in abbreviated form, not in full.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-032","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":32,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) ICBN\n(B) ICZN\n(C) Nomenclature\n(D) Identification\nList-II:\n(i) Code of rules for naming animals\n(ii) Correct description of an organism\n(iii) Code of rules for naming plants\n(iv) Standardised naming so that an organism has the same name worldwide","image":"","options":["A-iii, B-i, C-iv, D-ii","A-i, B-iii, C-ii, D-iv","A-iii, B-i, C-ii, D-iv","A-i, B-iii, C-iv, D-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) ICBN is the code for botanical (plant) names, so A-iii.\n(B) ICZN is the code for zoological (animal) names, so B-i.\n(C) Nomenclature standardises naming so that an organism has one worldwide name, so C-iv.\n(D) Identification is the correct description of an organism, so D-ii.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-033","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":33,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"A student writes the scientific name of the potato by hand in her notebook as Solanum tuberosum, with one continuous line drawn under both words together. The letters are not slanted. Which statement best evaluates her writing?","image":"","options":["Correct, because a handwritten name needs only to be underlined in some way","Incorrect, because handwritten names must also be in italics in addition to being underlined","Incorrect, because only the generic name should be underlined","Incorrect, because each of the two words must be underlined separately"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The capitalisation is correct: Solanum is capitalised and tuberosum begins with a small letter.\nNCERT specifies that, when handwritten, both words are separately underlined, so one continuous line breaks this rule.\nItalics are the printed alternative to underlining; they are not required in addition to it.\nUnderlining only the genus is also wrong.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-034","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":34,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"According to the taxonomic categories given in NCERT for common organisms, the housefly (Musca domestica) belongs to which order?","image":"","options":["Diptera","Muscidae","Insecta","Poales"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In NCERT Table 1.1 the housefly is classified as genus Musca, family Muscidae, order Diptera, class Insecta and phylum Arthropoda.\nMuscidae is tempting, but it is the housefly's family, not its order.\nInsecta is its class.\nPoales is the order of wheat, a plant.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-035","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":35,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"NCERT uses insects to explain how a group of organisms gets a rank in classification. Insects form a recognisable group because all of them share which common feature?","image":"","options":["Three pairs of jointed legs","Four pairs of jointed legs","Presence of a notochord","A dorsal hollow neural system"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT describes insects as a group sharing a common feature: three pairs of jointed legs.\nBecause they share this feature, insects are recognisable, concrete objects that can be classified and given a rank or category.\nFour pairs of jointed legs is a tempting distractor, but it is not the feature NCERT gives for insects.\nThe notochord and the dorsal hollow neural system are the features NCERT uses for phylum Chordata, not for insects.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-036","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":36,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Classification is a single-step process of placing an organism in one group.\n(ii) Hearing the term 'mammals' brings to mind animals with external ears and body hair.\n(iii) Taxonomic groups are merely morphological aggregates, not distinct biological entities.\n(iv) The lower the taxon, the more characteristics its members share.\n(v) Classifying all living organisms into different taxa based on characteristics is called taxonomy.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Incorrect. Classification involves a hierarchy of steps, and each step represents a rank or category.\n(ii) Correct. Naming a taxon makes us associate its characters with its members, such as external ears and body hair for mammals.\n(iii) Incorrect. NCERT states that taxonomic groups are distinct biological entities, not merely morphological aggregates.\n(iv) Correct. As we move from kingdom down to species, the members share more and more common characters.\n(v) Correct. This is how NCERT describes taxonomy.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-037","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":37,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The cat and the dog are both placed in the order Carnivora.\nReason (R): The cat and the dog are placed in the same family, Felidae.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. The order Carnivora includes the families Felidae (cats) and Canidae (dogs).\nR is false. Cats and dogs show some similarities but also differences, so they are kept in separate families: cat in Felidae and dog in Canidae.\nTwo animals can share an order without sharing a family.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-038","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":38,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"In the name Mangifera indica Linn., the abbreviation 'Linn.' indicates that:","image":"","options":["The species belongs to the Linnaean family of plants","The name was approved by ICZN","The species is found only in Europe","The species was first described by Linnaeus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The author's name comes at the end of the biological name, after the specific epithet, in an abbreviated form.\n'Linn.' therefore shows that Linnaeus first described this species.\nThere is no 'Linnaean family', and ICZN governs animal names, not plant names like mango.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-039","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":39,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, classification is not a single-step process. It involves a hierarchy of steps. What does each step in this hierarchy represent?","image":"","options":["A rank or category","A single species","A morphological aggregate","An ecological niche"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT says classification involves a hierarchy of steps, and each step represents a rank or category.\nBecause each category is part of the overall taxonomic arrangement, it is called a taxonomic category.\nA species is only one such rank, the lowest. It does not describe every step.\nNCERT also says taxonomic groups are not merely morphological aggregates.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-040","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":40,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Biological names are Latinised or derived from Latin, whatever their origin.\nStatement II: When a biological name is handwritten, the generic name and the specific epithet are underlined together with one continuous line.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I: Correct. NCERT says biological names are generally in Latin, or Latinised, irrespective of their origin.\nStatement II: Incorrect. In a handwritten name, the two words are underlined separately, not with one continuous line. The underlining shows their Latin origin.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-041","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":41,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Potato and brinjal belong to the same genus and are therefore also in the same family, Solanaceae.\n(ii) The lion and the domestic cat belong to different genera but to the same family.\n(iii) Convolvulaceae and Solanaceae are placed in the same order.\n(iv) The cat and the dog share the family Felidae because both are carnivores.\n(v) Members of the family Solanaceae share more characters among themselves than members of the order Polymoniales do.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: both are Solanum species, and Solanum is in Solanaceae.\n(ii) Correct: the lion is Panthera and the cat is Felis, and both are in Felidae.\n(iii) Correct: both families are in the order Polymoniales.\n(iv) Incorrect: the dog belongs to Canidae. Only their order, Carnivora, is common.\n(v) Correct: a lower taxon shares more characters than a higher one.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-042","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":42,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The figure of 1.7–1.8 million known species is not a final count of biodiversity on earth.\nReason (R): New organisms are continuously being identified as we explore new areas, and even old ones.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT presents 1.7–1.8 million as the number of species known and described so far. Many organisms still remain unknown.\nR is true: NCERT notes that new organisms keep being identified, in new areas as well as old ones.\nThis continuous discovery is exactly why the count is not final, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-043","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":43,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (taxon) with List-II (taxonomic category):\nList-I\na. Sapindales\nb. Dicotyledonae\nc. Angiospermae\nd. Convolvulaceae\nList-II\n(i) Family\n(ii) Division\n(iii) Order\n(iv) Class","image":"","options":["a-iii, b-iv, c-ii, d-i","a-i, b-iv, c-ii, d-iii","a-iii, b-ii, c-iv, d-i","a-iv, b-iii, c-ii, d-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Sapindales is the order of mango (Table 1.1), so a-iii.\nDicotyledonae is the class of mango, so b-iv.\nAngiospermae is the division of mango and wheat, so c-ii.\nConvolvulaceae is a plant family; the ending -aceae marks a family, and NCERT places it in the order Polymoniales. So d-i.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-044","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":44,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (scientific name) with List-II (common name):\nList-I\n(A) Panthera pardus\n(B) Solanum melongena\n(C) Solanum tuberosum\n(D) Panthera tigris\nList-II\n(I) Potato\n(II) Tiger\n(III) Leopard\n(IV) Brinjal","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Panthera pardus is the leopard, so A matches III.\n(B) Solanum melongena is brinjal, so B matches IV.\n(C) Solanum tuberosum is potato, so C matches I.\n(D) Panthera tigris is the tiger, so D matches II.\nThe correct combination is A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-045","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":45,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements about the higher taxonomic categories is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Class Mammalia includes orders other than Primata and Carnivora.","Tiger and gorilla are placed in the same class but in different orders.","The higher the category, the easier it becomes to determine its relationship to other taxa at the same level.","Kingdom Plantae comprises all plants from various divisions."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: NCERT states that class Mammalia has other orders as well.\n(2) Correct: tiger is in order Carnivora and gorilla is in order Primata, and both orders belong to class Mammalia.\n(3) Incorrect: NCERT says the higher the category, the greater the difficulty of determining relationships to other taxa at the same level. This makes classification more complex.\n(4) Correct: Kingdom Plantae is made up of plants from various divisions.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-046","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":46,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"The term ‘systematics’ refers to:","image":"","options":["Identification and study of organ systems of plants and animals","Identification and preservation of plants and animals","Diversity of kinds of organisms and their relationship","Study of habitats of organisms and their classification"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: systematics (from Latin 'systema', systematic arrangement) is the study of the diversity of kinds of organisms and their relationships, including evolutionary relationships.\nIt covers identification, nomenclature and classification.\nOption (2) is tempting, but it describes only part of the work. Preservation belongs to museums and herbaria.\nOptions (1) and (4) describe anatomy and ecology.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 1 (MCQ Q3)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-047","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":47,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"In the scientific name Panthera leo, what does the word 'leo' represent?","image":"","options":["The genus","The specific epithet","The family","The name of the author"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In a binomial name, the first word is the genus and the second word is the specific epithet.\nIn Panthera leo, Panthera is the genus and leo is the specific epithet for the lion.\nThe author's name, when given, comes after the specific epithet in abbreviated form, for example Linn.\nThe family is not part of the binomial name.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-048","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":48,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Nomenclature\n(b) Identification\n(c) Classification\n(d) Systematics\nList-II\n(i) Grouping into convenient categories based on easily observable characters\n(ii) Study of the kinds and diversity of organisms and the relationships among them\n(iii) Standardising names so that an organism has the same name worldwide\n(iv) Correct description of an organism so that a name can be attached to it","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Nomenclature is the standardised naming that gives each organism one worldwide name, so (iii).\n(b) Identification is the correct description of an organism, so (iv).\n(c) Classification groups organisms into convenient categories using easily observable characters, so (i).\n(d) Systematics studies the diversity of organisms and the relationships among them, so (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-049","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":49,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"A student lists the organisms she can see in her potted plants at home. She then repeats the survey in a large, dense forest. Based on NCERT, which conclusion is most appropriate?","image":"","options":["The number of kinds of organisms will remain the same, since species are found everywhere","The range and variety of organisms will increase, and each different kind she sees represents a species","Only the number of individuals will increase, not the number of kinds","She will see fewer kinds, because microscopic organisms are absent in forests"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT says that increasing the area of observation increases the range and variety of organisms seen.\nA dense forest shows a much greater number and kinds of organisms.\nEach different kind of plant, animal or organism represents a species.\nOption (3) fails because the number of kinds also increases. Option (4) fails because organisms invisible to the naked eye are present everywhere.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-050","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":50,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (taxon) with List-II (taxonomic category):\nList-I\n(a) Felidae\n(b) Carnivora\n(c) Mammalia\n(d) Chordata\nList-II\n(i) Phylum\n(ii) Class\n(iii) Order\n(iv) Family","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) Felidae is the family that contains Panthera and Felis, so it matches (iv).\n(b) Carnivora is the order that contains Felidae and Canidae, so it matches (iii).\n(c) Mammalia is the class that contains Primata and Carnivora, so it matches (ii).\n(d) Chordata is the phylum that contains fishes through mammals, so it matches (i).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-051","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":51,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The families Felidae and Canidae are both included in the order Carnivora.\nReason (R): The members of an order share a greater number of similar characters than the members of a family.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT places Felidae (cats) and Canidae (dogs) in the animal order Carnivora.\nR is false. An order is an assemblage of families that show only a few similar characters.\nThe number of shared characters decreases as we move higher from family to order, not the other way round.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-052","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":52,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Local names of plants and animals are not suitable for scientific communication mainly because they","image":"","options":["are always written in Latin and are hard to understand","vary from place to place, even within a country","are framed only under ICZN rules","always include the abbreviated name of the author"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT notes that we know the organisms of our own area by local names, and these names vary from place to place, even within a country. This causes confusion when people talk about organisms.\nLatin and italics are features of scientific names, not local names.\nICZN rules apply to scientific names of animals.\nThe author's name is added after scientific names, not local names.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-053","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":53,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Figure 1.1 of NCERT shows taxonomic categories arranged in ascending order, from species at the base to kingdom at the top. Which pair of trends is correct as one moves upward from species to kingdom?","image":"","options":["Common characteristics among members increase; difficulty of relating a taxon to other taxa at the same level decreases","Common characteristics among members decrease; difficulty of relating a taxon to other taxa at the same level decreases","Common characteristics among members decrease; difficulty of relating a taxon to other taxa at the same level increases","Common characteristics among members remain constant; difficulty of relating a taxon to other taxa at the same level increases"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Moving up from species to kingdom, each taxon contains more varied organisms. So the number of common characteristics shared by its members decreases.\nNCERT also states that the higher the category, the greater the difficulty of determining its relationship to other taxa at the same level.\nThis is why classification becomes more complex at higher levels.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-054","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":54,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Nomenclature of an organism is possible only after it has been identified.\nReason (R): Identification means describing an organism correctly, so that we know which organism a name is attached to.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT says naming is possible only when the organism is described correctly.\nR is true. Identification is the correct description that tells us which organism the name belongs to.\nA name cannot be attached to an organism that has not been correctly described. So R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-055","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":55,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Systematics takes into account the evolutionary relationships between organisms.\nReason (R): The word systematics is derived from the Latin word 'systema'.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true: systematics studies diversity and the relationships among organisms, including evolutionary relationships.\nR is true: 'systematics' comes from 'systema', meaning systematic arrangement.\nHowever, the origin of the word does not explain why systematics considers evolutionary relationships.\nSo both statements are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-056","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":56,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"In the taxonomic hierarchy, the order Primata includes monkey, gorilla and gibbon. In which class is this order placed?","image":"","options":["Class Aves","Class Mammalia","Class Reptilia","Class Insecta"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A class is a group of related orders.\nNCERT places order Primata (monkey, gorilla, gibbon) in class Mammalia.\nOrder Carnivora (tiger, cat, dog) is placed in the same class.\nAves and Reptilia are other classes of chordates. Insecta belongs to phylum Arthropoda. None of these contains primates.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-057","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":57,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"The word 'systematics' is derived from the Latin word 'systema'. What does 'systema' mean?","image":"","options":["Naming of organisms in two words","Study of the purpose of life","Systematic arrangement of organisms","Description of external features"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"'Systematics' comes from the Latin 'systema'.\n'Systema' means the systematic arrangement of organisms.\nNaming organisms in two words is binomial nomenclature, which is a different idea.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-058","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":58,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (Family) with List-II (Order):\nList-I\n(A) Felidae\n(B) Solanaceae\n(C) Muscidae\n(D) Poaceae\nList-II\n(i) Poales\n(ii) Diptera\n(iii) Carnivora\n(iv) Polymoniales","image":"","options":["A-iii, B-iv, C-ii, D-i","A-iii, B-i, C-ii, D-iv","A-ii, B-iv, C-iii, D-i","A-iv, B-iii, C-i, D-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Felidae (cats, lion, tiger) belongs to the order Carnivora, so A-iii.\n(B) Solanaceae is placed in Polymoniales on the basis of floral characters, so B-iv.\n(C) Muscidae (housefly) belongs to the order Diptera, so C-ii.\n(D) Poaceae (wheat) belongs to the order Poales, so D-i.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-059","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":59,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"When we hear the term 'mammals', which pair of characters, as mentioned in NCERT, immediately comes to mind?","image":"","options":["Three pairs of jointed legs and wings","Scales and fins","Feathers and a beak","External ears and body hair"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT notes that a taxon name brings to mind the characters linked with that group.\nFor 'mammals', we think of animals with external ears and body hair.\nThree pairs of jointed legs belong to insects.\nScales and fins suggest fishes, and feathers and a beak suggest birds.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-060","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":60,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Using NCERT's examples, which pair of organisms shares the lowest (most specific) common taxonomic category?","image":"","options":["Man and lion","Mango and wheat","Lion and cat","Man and housefly"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Lion and cat: both are in family Felidae (genera Panthera and Felis).\nMan and lion: they share only class Mammalia (orders Primata and Carnivora).\nMango and wheat: they share only division Angiospermae.\nMan and housefly: they share only the kingdom Animalia.\nFamily is the lowest of these shared categories.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-061","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":61,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about binomial nomenclature are correct?\n(i) Biological names are Latinised or derived from Latin, whatever their origin.\n(ii) The generic name begins with a capital letter.\n(iii) The specific epithet begins with a capital letter.\n(iv) Printed biological names are written in italics.\n(v) The author's name is written in full before the generic name.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: names are Latinised irrespective of their origin.\n(ii) Correct: the genus starts with a capital letter.\n(iii) Incorrect: the specific epithet starts with a small letter.\n(iv) Correct: printed names are in italics.\n(v) Incorrect: the author's name comes after the specific epithet, in abbreviated form.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-062","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":62,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following can be called taxa as per NCERT?\nPlants, Wheat, Mammals, Dogs, Animals, Rice","image":"","options":["Three","Four","Five","Six"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT lists Dogs, Cats, Mammals, Wheat, Rice, Plants and Animals as convenient categories, that is, taxa.\nAll six terms given here appear in that list.\nThey differ only in their level. For example, 'Plants' is very broad, while 'Wheat' is narrow.\nSo all six are taxa.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-063","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":63,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Potato (Solanum), Petunia and Datura are three different genera that share enough features to be grouped together in one family. Which family is it?","image":"","options":["Convolvulaceae","Solanaceae","Anacardiaceae","Poaceae"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT gives Solanum, Petunia and Datura as three different genera placed in the family Solanaceae.\nConvolvulaceae is a separate family. It is grouped with Solanaceae only at the level of order (Polymoniales), so it is the most tempting wrong option.\nAnacardiaceae is the family of mango, and Poaceae is the family of wheat.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-064","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":64,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Ernst Mayr won three prizes widely regarded as the 'triple crown of biology'. Which of the following is NOT one of them?","image":"","options":["Balzan Prize","International Prize for Biology","Crafoord Prize","Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The triple crown of biology won by Mayr consists of three prizes:\nthe Balzan Prize (1983),\nthe International Prize for Biology (1994),\nand the Crafoord Prize (1999).\nThe Nobel Prize is not part of this triple crown.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-065","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":65,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"All animals belonging to various phyla are assigned to which taxonomic category?","image":"","options":["Kingdom Animalia, the highest category","Class Mammalia, the highest category","Phylum Chordata, the highest category","Division Animalia, the highest category"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Kingdom is the highest taxonomic category.\nAll animals from the various phyla are placed in Kingdom Animalia.\nIn the same way, all plants from the various divisions form Kingdom Plantae.\nClass and phylum are lower ranks than kingdom.\n'Division' is a plant category below kingdom, so it cannot hold all animals.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-066","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":66,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"The genus Panthera, which includes the lion, tiger and leopard, is distinguished from another genus that includes cats. Name that genus.","image":"","options":["Canis","Homo","Felis","Musca"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the genus Panthera differs from the genus Felis, which includes cats.\nBoth genera are placed together in the family Felidae.\nCanis would be a tempting choice, but dogs belong to the family Canidae, not Felidae.\nHomo is the genus of human beings, and Musca is the genus of the housefly.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-067","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":67,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly compares how many characters a lion shares with a tiger and with a domestic cat?","image":"","options":["The lion shares more characters with the cat, because both are placed in the family Felidae","The lion shares more characters with the tiger, because both belong to the genus Panthera, while the cat belongs to the genus Felis","The lion shares equal characters with both, because all three belong to the family Felidae","The lion shares more characters with the cat, because Felis and Panthera are the same genus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The lower the taxon, the more characters its members share.\nThe lion and tiger are in the same genus (Panthera), which is a lower category than family.\nThe cat is in the genus Felis, and it is grouped with Panthera only at the family level (Felidae).\nSo the lion shares more characters with the tiger than with the cat.\nOption (3) fails because sharing a family does not mean equal similarity.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-068","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":68,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"The two components of a binomial scientific name are:","image":"","options":["Family name and generic name","Generic name and specific epithet","Specific epithet and author name","Order name and specific epithet"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Each scientific name has two components: the generic name and the specific epithet. That is why the system is called binomial.\nThe author's name may follow in abbreviated form, but it is not one of the two components.\nFamily and order are higher categories and are not part of the name.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-069","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":69,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Mango and wheat are placed in the same division but in different classes.\nStatement II: Man and housefly are placed in the same phylum but in different classes.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Both mango and wheat are in division Angiospermae. Mango's class is Dicotyledonae, while wheat's class is Monocotyledonae.\nStatement II is incorrect. Man belongs to phylum Chordata, but the housefly belongs to phylum Arthropoda.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-070","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":70,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Which taxonomic category is the lowest category for all organisms, including those in the plant and animal kingdoms?","image":"","options":["Genus","Family","Species","Order"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that all organisms, including plants and animals, have species as the lowest category.\nGenus is one step higher. It is an aggregate of closely related species.\nFamily and order are higher still.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-071","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":71,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, classification is the process by which anything is grouped into convenient categories based on:","image":"","options":["DNA sequences of organisms only","Easily observable characters","The economic value of organisms only","The geographical location of organisms"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT defines classification as grouping anything into convenient categories based on some easily observable characters.\nExamples are plants, animals, dogs, cats and insects. Each is recognised by visible features.\nDNA sequences are not the basis given in this definition.\nUses and location are also not the basis of this definition.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-072","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":72,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"The principles and criteria for giving scientific names to plants are provided in which of the following?","image":"","options":["International Code of Zoological Nomenclature (ICZN)","International Code for Botanical Nomenclature (ICBN)","Systema Naturae","Taxonomic hierarchy of Linnaeus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that scientific names of plants are based on agreed principles and criteria provided in the International Code for Botanical Nomenclature (ICBN).\nICZN is the code that animal taxonomists use, so option (1) is the tempting wrong choice.\nSystema Naturae is the title of a publication by Linnaeus, not a naming code.\nThe taxonomic hierarchy is a set of ranks, not a set of naming rules.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-073","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":73,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"In NCERT Table 1.1, mango (Mangifera indica) is placed in which family?","image":"","options":["Sapindales","Anacardiaceae","Poaceae","Solanaceae"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Table 1.1 classifies mango as follows: genus Mangifera, family Anacardiaceae, order Sapindales, class Dicotyledonae, division Angiospermae.\nSapindales is its order, not its family.\nPoaceae is the family of wheat.\nSolanaceae contains Solanum, Petunia and Datura.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-074","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":74,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are ensured by scientific names, as described in NCERT?\n(a) Each organism has only one name\n(b) The name has not been used for any other known organism\n(c) The description allows people anywhere in the world to arrive at the same name\n(d) The name matches the local name used in the organism's native region\n(e) The name changes according to the language of the country","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Ensured: each organism has only one scientific name.\n(b) Ensured: the name is not used for any other known organism.\n(c) Ensured: the description should lead people anywhere to the same name.\n(d) Not ensured: scientific names are Latinised and replace variable local names.\n(e) Not ensured: a name that changed with language would defeat the purpose of standardisation.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-075","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":75,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: As scientists, biologists try to answer what the purpose of life is.\nStatement II: Biodiversity refers to the number and types of organisms present on earth.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. The purpose of life is the philosophical question, and NCERT says scientists will not attempt to answer it. They reflect only on what is living.\nStatement II is correct. NCERT defines biodiversity as the number and types of organisms present on earth.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-076","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":76,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): 'Wheat' and 'Plants' are both taxa.\nReason (R): Only a group at the level of species can be called a taxon.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT explicitly states that 'Plants' forms a taxon and 'Wheat' is also a taxon.\nR is false. A taxon can be a category at any level, from species up to kingdom, not just species.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-077","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":77,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Wheat (Triticum aestivum) is placed in which class according to NCERT Table 1.1?","image":"","options":["Dicotyledonae","Poales","Angiospermae","Monocotyledonae"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"In Table 1.1, wheat's class is Monocotyledonae.\nDicotyledonae is the class of mango, which is a tempting distractor.\nPoales is wheat's order.\nAngiospermae is wheat's division.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-078","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":78,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Which branch of study deals with the kinds and diversity of organisms and the relationships among them, and takes evolutionary relationships into account?","image":"","options":["Nomenclature","Identification","Systematics","Characterisation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Systematics comes from the Latin word 'systema', meaning systematic arrangement of organisms. Linnaeus titled his publication Systema Naturae.\nIts scope was later enlarged to include identification, nomenclature and classification. It also considers evolutionary relationships between organisms.\nNomenclature, identification and characterisation are only individual processes within this broader field.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-079","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":79,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Members of a kingdom share fewer common characters than members of a genus.\nReason (R): As we move up the taxonomic hierarchy from species to kingdom, the number of common characteristics goes on decreasing.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. A genus is a group of closely related species, while a kingdom groups many phyla or divisions that have little in common.\nR is true. NCERT states that the number of shared characters decreases as we go higher from species to kingdom.\nR directly explains A: kingdom sits above genus in the hierarchy, so its members share fewer characters.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-080","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":80,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements is correct according to NCERT's introduction to diversity in the living world?","image":"","options":["Societies with an anthropocentric view of biology made rapid progress in biological knowledge","Early man deified only certain animals and plants, never inanimate matter","Recognising that present-day organisms are related to each other and to all organisms that ever lived led to cultural movements for conservation of biodiversity","Detailed systems of identification, nomenclature and classification developed as a luxury rather than out of necessity"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: societies with an anthropocentric view registered only limited progress.\n(2) Incorrect: early man deified inanimate matter such as wind, sea and fire, as well as some animals and plants.\n(3) Correct: recognising this relatedness humbled man and led to conservation movements.\n(4) Incorrect: these systems were brought in out of necessity.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-081","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":81,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A tiger and a gorilla belong to the same class but to different orders, so they share fewer common characteristics than a tiger and a cat.\nStatement II: The lower the taxon, the more characteristics its members share; the number of common characteristics decreases as we go from species to kingdom.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The tiger is in order Carnivora and the gorilla is in order Primata. Both orders are placed in class Mammalia, so the lowest category the two animals share is the class.\nThe tiger (genus Panthera) and the cat (genus Felis) are both in family Felidae. Family is a lower taxon than class.\nStatement II is correct: lower taxa share more characteristics, and common characters decrease from species to kingdom.\nSo the tiger and cat share more characters than the tiger and gorilla, and Statement I is correct.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-082","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":82,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Botanical gardens and zoological parks have","image":"","options":["Collection of endemic living species only","Collection of exotic living species only","Collection of endemic and exotic living species","Collection of only local plants and animals"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: botanical gardens and zoological parks keep living organisms for reference, study and conservation.\nThey hold species native to the region (endemic or local) and also species brought from other regions and countries (exotic).\nTheir purpose is to show a wide range of diversity, so options limiting them to only one kind are wrong.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 1 (MCQ Q6)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-083","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":83,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Two animals are placed in the same order but in different families. Which conclusion about them is valid?","image":"","options":["They must belong to the same genus","They share more characters than two species of the same genus","They also belong to the same class and phylum","They may belong to different kingdoms"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"An order is an assemblage of families. Every higher category, such as class, phylum and kingdom, contains the whole order.\nSo the two animals must share the same class, phylum and kingdom. This rules out option (4).\nThey are in different families, so they cannot be in the same genus, which rules out option (1).\nMembers of a lower taxon such as a genus share more characters, so option (2) is wrong.\nExample: cat (Felidae) and dog (Canidae) are both in order Carnivora and class Mammalia.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-084","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":84,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the scientific naming of organisms is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Scientific names ensure that each organism has only one name.","Names of plants follow ICBN, while names of animals follow ICZN.","In a binomial, the specific epithet is written first, followed by the generic name.","Biological names are Latinised or derived from Latin irrespective of their origin."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: scientific names give each organism a single name.\n(2) Correct: plant names follow the International Code for Botanical Nomenclature (ICBN). Animal names follow the International Code of Zoological Nomenclature (ICZN).\n(3) Incorrect: the generic name comes first and the specific epithet second, as in Mangifera indica.\n(4) Correct: names are Latinised whatever their origin.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-085","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":85,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"According to the taxonomic categories of common organisms given in NCERT (Table 1.1), which pair of organisms belongs to the same Phylum/Division?","image":"","options":["Man and housefly","Mango and wheat","Housefly and wheat","Man and mango"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Man is in Chordata and housefly is in Arthropoda, so they are in different phyla.\nMango and wheat are both placed in the division Angiospermae.\nThey separate only at the class level: Dicotyledonae for mango and Monocotyledonae for wheat.\nThe other pairs mix an animal phylum with a plant division, or two different phyla.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-086","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":86,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT Table 1.1, the housefly (Musca domestica) belongs to which order?","image":"","options":["Muscidae","Insecta","Diptera","Arthropoda"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Table 1.1 places the housefly as genus Musca, family Muscidae, order Diptera, class Insecta and phylum Arthropoda.\nMuscidae is its family, which ranks below the order.\nInsecta is its class and Arthropoda is its phylum. Both rank above the order.\nSo the order is Diptera.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-087","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":87,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Match each taxon in List-I with the next higher taxon in which NCERT places it (List-II).\nList-I:\nA. Datura\nB. Canidae\nC. Primata\nD. Insecta\nList-II:\n(i) Arthropoda\n(ii) Solanaceae\n(iii) Carnivora\n(iv) Mammalia","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A. Datura is a genus. It is placed in the family Solanaceae along with Solanum and Petunia, so A-(ii).\nB. Canidae is a family. It is placed in the order Carnivora along with Felidae, so B-(iii).\nC. Primata is an order. It is placed in the class Mammalia, so C-(iv).\nD. Insecta is a class. The housefly's class falls under the phylum Arthropoda, so D-(i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-088","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":88,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about taxonomic categories is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["A category is called a taxonomic category because it is part of the overall taxonomic arrangement","Insects were given a rank because they are recognisable concrete objects that can be classified","The higher the category, the easier it is to determine its relationship to other taxa at the same level","Knowledge of characters helps identify similarities and dissimilarities among individuals of the same kind and of other kinds"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: this is NCERT's reason for the term 'taxonomic category'.\n(2) Correct: insects are recognisable concrete objects, so they were given a rank.\n(3) Incorrect: the higher the category, the greater the difficulty of determining relationships at the same level. This makes classification more complex.\n(4) Correct: knowledge of characters is the basic requirement for placing organisms in categories.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-089","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":89,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A scientific name can be assigned to an organism only after the organism has been correctly described.\nStatement II: Names of animals are framed under ICBN, the same code that governs names of plants.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: nomenclature is possible only after identification, that is, correct description of the organism.\nStatement II is incorrect: animal taxonomists have evolved a separate code, the International Code of Zoological Nomenclature (ICZN).\nICBN applies to plants only.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-090","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":90,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Since a dog is a mammal and mammals are animals, 'dogs' is a lower-level taxon than 'animals'","Using the term 'cats' makes us associate certain characters with all organisms of that group","'Wheat' and 'rice' are taxa, but 'plants' is too broad a group to be called a taxon","As we move to higher taxa, the members share fewer common characteristics"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct. Dogs are included within mammals, which are included within animals, so 'dogs' is the lower taxon.\n(2) Correct. NCERT notes that naming a group makes us associate its characters with its members.\n(3) Incorrect. NCERT clearly says 'Plants' also forms a taxa. Broad groups are taxa at a higher level.\n(4) Correct. Higher categories share fewer characters.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-091","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":91,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Which title did Carolus Linnaeus use for his publication, reflecting the Latin root of the word systematics?","image":"","options":["Origin of Species","Species Plantarum Indica","Historia Animalium","Systema Naturae"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT notes that Linnaeus titled his publication Systema Naturae.\nThe title uses 'systema', the Latin root of systematics, meaning systematic arrangement.\nOrigin of Species was written by Darwin, not Linnaeus.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-092","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":92,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The plant families Convolvulaceae and Solanaceae are placed in the same order, Polymoniales.\nReason (R): Orders are grouped mainly on aggregates of characters, and Polymoniales is defined chiefly by floral characters.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT names Convolvulaceae and Solanaceae as families of the order Polymoniales.\nR is true. Order and higher categories are identified on aggregates of characters, and these two families are grouped mainly on floral characters.\nR explains why the two families share an order.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-093","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":93,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"NCERT uses insects to illustrate a taxonomic category. Which common feature shared by insects is cited to show that they form a recognisable group?","image":"","options":["Two pairs of jointed legs","Three pairs of jointed legs","Four pairs of jointed legs","Presence of notochord"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Insects are described as a group of organisms sharing common features such as three pairs of jointed legs.\nThis makes them recognisable concrete objects that can be classified and given a rank.\nA notochord is the feature used for phylum Chordata, not for insects.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-094","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":94,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"A forest officer sorts the trees of a region into three groups: timber-yielding, fuelwood and fruit-bearing. Which statement best describes this grouping?","image":"","options":["It is systematics, because it arranges organisms in an orderly way.","It is modern taxonomy, because forest ecology is involved.","It is nomenclature, because each group receives a name.","It resembles the earliest use-based classifications and does not reflect evolutionary relationships."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The trees are grouped by their usefulness to humans, as the earliest human classifications were.\nSystematics considers evolutionary relationships. Trees of very different lineages could end up in the same use group, so (1) fails.\nModern taxonomy relies on the organisms' own structure, cells, development and ecology, not on human uses, so (2) fails.\nNomenclature gives standardised scientific names to individual organisms, not labels to use groups, so (3) fails.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-095","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":95,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"A student studies four plants: potato, brinjal, Petunia, and a plant from the family Convolvulaceae. What is the lowest taxonomic category that includes all four plants?","image":"","options":["Genus Solanum","Family Solanaceae","Order Polymoniales","Division Angiospermae"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Potato and brinjal are both species of the genus Solanum.\nAdding Petunia requires the family level, Solanaceae.\nThe Convolvulaceae member lies outside Solanaceae. Convolvulaceae and Solanaceae are united only in the order Polymoniales.\nAngiospermae also contains all four plants, but it is a higher category, not the lowest.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-096","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":96,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Systematics takes into account evolutionary relationships between organisms.\nStatement II: The scope of systematics was later enlarged to include identification, nomenclature and classification.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT states that systematics considers evolutionary relationships between organisms.\nStatement II is correct. Systematics began as the study of the diversity of organisms and the relationships among them. Its scope was later widened to cover identification, nomenclature and classification.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-097","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":97,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (taxon) with List-II (taxonomic category):\nList-I: (A) Felidae (B) Poales (C) Dicotyledonae (D) Angiospermae\nList-II: (i) Class (ii) Division (iii) Family (iv) Order","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Felidae is a family. It contains the genera Panthera and Felis.\n(B) Poales is the order of wheat.\n(C) Dicotyledonae is the class of mango.\n(D) Angiospermae is the division of both mango and wheat.\nSo the correct matching is A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-098","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":98,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following words are specific epithets?\nindica, Solanum, tuberosum, Panthera, leo, sapiens, Homo, nigrum","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Specific epithets are the second words of binomial names and begin with a small letter.\nThe specific epithets in the list are indica, tuberosum, leo, sapiens and nigrum, which makes 5.\nSolanum, Panthera and Homo are generic names, and they begin with a capital letter.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-099","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":99,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Taxonomic groups or categories are distinct biological entities and not merely morphological aggregates.\nStatement II: Taxonomists have developed sub-categories within the broad hierarchy to allow more sound and scientific placement of taxa.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT explicitly calls taxonomic groups distinct biological entities, not merely morphological aggregates.\nStatement II is correct. Species to kingdom are broad categories, and sub-categories were developed to place taxa more soundly and scientifically.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-100","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":100,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"In taxonomic studies, a group of individual organisms showing fundamental similarities is considered a:","image":"","options":["Genus","Family","Species","Order"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT defines a species as a group of individual organisms with fundamental similarities.\nA species should be distinguishable from closely related species by distinct morphological differences.\nA genus is an aggregate of related species, so it is a higher category than species.\nFamily and order are higher still, and their members share fewer similarities.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-101","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":101,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) All taxonomic categories together constitute the taxonomic hierarchy.\n(ii) Each category is referred to as a unit of classification.\n(iii) The plural of taxon is taxa.\n(iv) Kingdom is the lowest taxonomic category.\n(v) A category denotes a rank.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: all categories together form the taxonomic hierarchy.\n(ii) Correct: each category is a unit of classification.\n(iii) Correct: taxon (pl.: taxa).\n(iv) Incorrect: kingdom is the highest category, and species is the lowest.\n(v) Correct: groups represent categories, and a category denotes a rank.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-102","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":102,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"The plant families Convolvulaceae and Solanaceae are grouped together in the order Polymoniales. On what basis are they mainly grouped?","image":"","options":["Floral characters","Root characters","Leaf venation only","Habitat in which the plants grow"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT states that Convolvulaceae and Solanaceae are included in the order Polymoniales mainly on the basis of floral characters.\nFloral (reproductive) features are more stable and reliable than features such as roots, leaf venation or habitat.\nHabitat is an ecological detail, not the basis given for this order.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-103","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":103,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Fishes, amphibians, reptiles, birds and mammals are all placed in phylum Chordata. On what common features is this grouping based?","image":"","options":["Three pairs of jointed legs and a segmented body","Presence of notochord and dorsal hollow neural system","External ears and body hair","Similar floral and reproductive characters"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT places classes such as fishes, amphibians, reptiles, birds and mammals in phylum Chordata.\nThe common features used for this grouping are the presence of a notochord and a dorsal hollow neural system.\nThree pairs of jointed legs is the feature NCERT uses for insects.\nExternal ears and body hair describe mammals only, so they cannot unite all the chordate classes.\nFloral characters are used for plant orders, not animal phyla.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-104","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":104,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following pairs of animals:\nP: Lion and cat\nQ: Lion and tiger\nR: Gorilla and tiger\nS: Cat and dog\nArrange them in decreasing order of the number of characters they are expected to share, based on the lowest taxonomic category common to each pair.","image":"","options":["P > Q > S > R","Q > S > P > R","Q > P > R > S","Q > P > S > R"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Q: lion and tiger share a genus (Panthera).\nP: lion (Panthera) and cat (Felis) share a family (Felidae).\nS: cat (Felidae) and dog (Canidae) share an order (Carnivora).\nR: gorilla (Primata) and tiger (Carnivora) share only a class (Mammalia).\nThe lower the shared category, the more characters the pair shares. So the order is Q > P > S > R.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-105","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":105,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Species is the lowest category in both the plant and animal kingdoms.\n(ii) In plants, the category 'Division' is used in place of phylum.\n(iii) An order is an assemblage of genera that show a few similar characters.\n(iv) The author's name is written in abbreviated form before the generic name.\n(v) Taxonomic categories are distinct biological entities, not merely morphological aggregates.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: species is the lowest category for all organisms.\n(ii) Correct: plants use division instead of phylum.\n(iii) Incorrect: an order is an assemblage of families, not genera.\n(iv) Incorrect: the author's name comes after the specific epithet, e.g. Mangifera indica Linn.\n(v) Correct: this is stated directly in NCERT.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-106","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":106,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the taxonomic category 'family' is correct?","image":"","options":["In plants, families are characterised on the basis of both vegetative and reproductive features","Members of a family share more similarities than members of a single genus","Petunia and Datura belong to two different families","Panthera and Felis are placed in different families"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: NCERT states that plant families are characterised on the basis of both vegetative and reproductive features.\n(2) Incorrect: a family is a group of related genera with fewer similarities than a genus.\n(3) Incorrect: Petunia and Datura are both in Solanaceae.\n(4) Incorrect: Panthera and Felis are both in Felidae.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-107","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":107,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Based on the categories they share, which of the following pairs of organisms is expected to have the greatest number of common characteristics?","image":"","options":["Potato and brinjal","Potato and Petunia","Tiger and dog","Monkey and gibbon"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Potato and brinjal: both are species of the same genus, Solanum.\nPotato and Petunia: they share only the family Solanaceae.\nTiger and dog: they share only the order Carnivora, since their families are Felidae and Canidae.\nMonkey and gibbon: NCERT only places them together in order Primata.\nThe lower the shared category, the more characters the organisms have in common. Sharing a genus therefore means the most common characters.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-108","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":108,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (family) with List-II (organism):\nList-I\n(A) Hominidae\n(B) Muscidae\n(C) Anacardiaceae\n(D) Poaceae\nList-II\n(i) Wheat\n(ii) Mango\n(iii) Man\n(iv) Housefly","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Hominidae is the family of man (Homo sapiens), so A-(iii).\n(B) Muscidae is the family of the housefly (Musca domestica), so B-(iv).\n(C) Anacardiaceae is the family of mango (Mangifera indica), so C-(ii).\n(D) Poaceae is the family of wheat (Triticum aestivum), so D-(i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-109","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":109,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Based on NCERT's classification of man, housefly, mango and wheat, which of the following statements is correct?","image":"","options":["Mango and wheat belong to the same order but are placed in different families","Mango and wheat are expected to share more common characters with each other than man shares with the housefly","In the biological name of the housefly, both the genus and the specific epithet are Musca","Order Poales is a higher taxonomic category than class Monocotyledonae"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect. Mango is in order Sapindales and wheat is in order Poales, so they already differ at the order level.\n(2) Correct. Mango and wheat share a division (Angiospermae). Man and the housefly share only the kingdom (Animalia). The lower the shared taxon, the more characters the members share.\n(3) Incorrect. The housefly's name is Musca domestica, so its specific epithet is domestica.\n(4) Incorrect. A class is a higher category than an order, so Monocotyledonae ranks above Poales.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-110","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":110,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Using the NCERT table of taxonomic categories for man, housefly, mango and wheat, which of the following inferences is correct?","image":"","options":["Man and housefly belong to the same phylum but to different classes","Mango and wheat share the same division but differ from the class level downward, so they share fewer characters than mango shares with another member of the order Sapindales","Mango (Anacardiaceae) and wheat (Poaceae) belong to different families but to the same order","In Triticum aestivum, Triticum is the specific epithet and the family is Triticaceae"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: man is in Chordata and housefly is in Arthropoda, so they differ in phylum.\n(2) Correct: both are in Angiospermae, but mango is a dicot and wheat is a monocot. Members of a lower common taxon (the same order) share more characters, so mango is closer to another member of Sapindales.\n(3) Incorrect: mango is in the order Sapindales and wheat is in Poales.\n(4) Incorrect: Triticum is the genus, and the family is Poaceae.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-111","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":111,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, what is the main reason biologists needed a standardised system for naming living organisms?","image":"","options":["Local names of an organism vary from place to place, even within the same country, which causes confusion.","Local names are always in Latin, which most people cannot understand.","Every organism has the same local name worldwide, so it cannot be told apart from related forms.","Local names give the genus but not the specific epithet."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"People know organisms by local names, and these names change from place to place, even within one country.\nThis creates confusion when people try to refer to the same organism.\nStandardised naming (nomenclature) gives each organism one name used all over the world.\nOption 2 is wrong because Latin is a feature of scientific names, not local names.\nOption 3 is the opposite of the real situation.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-112","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":112,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following genus–family pairs are correctly matched?\nPanthera – Felidae\nDatura – Solanaceae\nMangifera – Anacardiaceae\nMusca – Muscidae\nTriticum – Poaceae\nFelis – Canidae\nHomo – Primata","image":"","options":["Three","Four","Five","Six"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Correct pairs: Panthera–Felidae, Datura–Solanaceae, Mangifera–Anacardiaceae, Musca–Muscidae and Triticum–Poaceae. That makes five.\nFelis (cats) belongs to Felidae, not Canidae, so this pair is wrong.\nPrimata is an order. The family of Homo is Hominidae, so this pair is also wrong.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-113","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":113,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"In the scientific name of the lion, Panthera leo, which component is the specific epithet?","image":"","options":["Panthera","leo","Felidae","Carnivora"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In a binomial name, the first word is the genus and the second word is the specific epithet.\nIn Panthera leo, Panthera is the genus and leo is the specific epithet.\nFelidae is the family and Carnivora is the order of the lion. They are not part of the binomial.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-114","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":114,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"All living organisms are linked to one another because","image":"","options":["They have common genetic material of the same type","They share common genetic material but to varying degrees","All have common cellular organization","All of above"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact (NCERT): all living organisms, past, present and future, are linked because they share common genetic material, but to varying degrees.\nOption (1) is the tempting trap. The genetic material is common, but it is not shared identically by all organisms.\nClosely related organisms share more of it than distant ones.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 1 (MCQ Q8)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-115","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":115,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"The question 'what is life?' contains two implicit questions. The technical one, which scientists attempt to answer, seeks to know:","image":"","options":["What the purpose of life is","What living is as opposed to the non-living","Why life originated on earth","Whether all organisms have equal value"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT splits 'what is life?' into two questions.\nThe technical question asks what living is as opposed to non-living.\nThe philosophical question asks what the purpose of life is. Scientists do not attempt to answer it.\nOption (1) is therefore the philosophical question, not the technical one.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-116","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":116,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Ernst Mayr, the evolutionary biologist at Harvard University, has been called:","image":"","options":["The Father of Taxonomy","The Darwin of the 20th century","The Father of Binomial Nomenclature","The Linnaeus of Zoology"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Ernst Mayr (1904–2004) worked at Harvard and has been called 'The Darwin of the 20th century'.\nHe made the origin of species diversity the central question of evolutionary biology.\nBinomial nomenclature was given by Carolus Linnaeus, not by Mayr. That rules out the tempting option (3).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-117","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":117,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Which pair of organisms from NCERT Table 1.1 belongs to the same phylum/division?","image":"","options":["Man and housefly","Mango and wheat","Man and mango","Housefly and wheat"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Both mango and wheat are placed in the division Angiospermae.\nMan is in phylum Chordata, while the housefly is in phylum Arthropoda, so that pair differs.\nThe other two pairs mix a plant with an animal, so they do not even share a kingdom.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-118","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":118,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The word systematics comes from the Latin word 'systema', meaning systematic arrangement of organisms.\n(ii) Linnaeus used Systema Naturae as the title of his publication.\n(iii) Systematics takes into account evolutionary relationships between organisms.\n(iv) The earliest classifications made by humans were based on evolutionary relationships among organisms.\n(v) Characterisation, identification, classification and nomenclature are the basic processes of taxonomy.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct. This is the origin of the word systematics.\n(ii) Correct. Linnaeus titled his publication Systema Naturae.\n(iii) Correct. Systematics considers evolutionary relationships between organisms.\n(iv) Incorrect. The earliest classifications were based on the 'uses' of organisms for food, clothing and shelter.\n(v) Correct. These four processes are basic to taxonomy.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-119","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":119,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the taxonomic hierarchy are correct?\n(i) As we move from species to kingdom, the number of common characteristics goes on decreasing.\n(ii) The lower the taxon, the more characteristics its members share.\n(iii) The higher the category, the easier it is to determine its relationship to other taxa at the same level.\n(iv) In plants, families are characterised on the basis of both vegetative and reproductive features.\n(v) Order and other higher categories are generally identified based on aggregates of characters.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: common characters decrease as we go upward from species to kingdom.\n(ii) Correct: members of lower taxa share more characters.\n(iii) Incorrect: the higher the category, the greater the difficulty of determining relationships, which makes classification more complex.\n(iv) Correct, as stated by NCERT.\n(v) Correct, as stated by NCERT.\nSo four statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-120","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":120,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Every genus must contain at least two species.\nStatement II: The different species placed in one genus show morphological similarities.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect: NCERT says each genus may have one or more than one specific epithet, so a genus with a single species is possible.\nStatement II is correct: the species within a genus represent different organisms but show morphological similarities, as seen in Panthera and Solanum.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-121","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":121,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the taxonomic hierarchy is correct?","image":"","options":["The higher the category, the easier it is to determine its relationship to other taxa at the same level","The lower the taxon, the more characteristics its members share","Orders are identified mainly on vegetative features, while families are identified on aggregates of characters","Species is the lowest category in the animal kingdom, but not in the plant kingdom"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect. The higher the category, the more difficult it is to determine its relationship to other taxa at the same level.\n(2) Correct. As we go down the hierarchy, members of a taxon share more characteristics.\n(3) Incorrect. Families are characterised on both vegetative and reproductive features, while orders are identified on aggregates of characters.\n(4) Incorrect. Species is the lowest category for all organisms, plants included.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-122","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":122,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, what were the earliest classifications of living organisms based on?","image":"","options":["Evolutionary relationships among organisms","Uses of organisms for basic human needs such as food, clothing and shelter","Internal cell structure of organisms","Developmental processes of organisms"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Early humans needed to find sources of food, clothing and shelter.\nSo the earliest classifications grouped organisms by their 'uses' to humans.\nEvolutionary relationships became important much later, with systematics.\nCell structure and development are bases of modern taxonomy, not early classification.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-123","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":123,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: 'Animals', 'mammals' and 'dogs' are all taxa.\nStatement II: 'Animals', 'mammals' and 'dogs' are taxa at the same level, because each of them is a group of organisms.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT names animals, mammals and dogs as taxa.\nStatement II is incorrect. A dog is a mammal and mammals are animals, so these three taxa are at different levels.\nBeing a group of organisms does not make taxa equal in rank.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-124","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":124,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements regarding binomial nomenclature is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["A scientific name ensures that the same name has not been used for any other known organism","Latinised names are given only to organisms first discovered in Latin-speaking regions","One generic name can be shared by more than one species, e.g. Panthera leo and Panthera tigris","A correct description of an organism should allow people anywhere in the world to arrive at the same name"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: scientific names ensure that each organism has one name and that the name is not used for any other known organism.\n(2) Incorrect: biological names are Latinised or derived from Latin irrespective of their origin, not only for organisms from Latin-speaking regions.\n(3) Correct: a genus can contain several specific epithets, e.g. leo and tigris.\n(4) Correct: a proper description should lead people anywhere to the same name.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-125","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":125,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following pairs of organisms is expected to share the greatest number of common characteristics?","image":"","options":["Panthera leo and Felis (domestic cat)","Panthera leo and Panthera tigris","Solanum tuberosum and Petunia","Homo sapiens and Panthera leo"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key idea: the lower the shared taxon, the more characteristics its members have in common.\nP. leo and P. tigris belong to the same genus, Panthera. This is the lowest shared rank among the options.\nP. leo and Felis share only the family Felidae.\nSolanum and Petunia also share only a family, Solanaceae.\nHomo sapiens and P. leo share only the class Mammalia, so they share the fewest characters.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-126","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":126,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Members of the same genus share more characteristics with each other than members of the same family do.\nReason (R): The lower the taxon, the more characteristics its members share.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. A genus ranks below a family, so its members have more characters in common.\nR is true. NCERT states that lower the taxa, more are the characteristics its members share.\nR explains A directly. Genus is the lower taxon, so its members share more characters.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-127","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":127,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"The plant families Convolvulaceae and Solanaceae are included in the order Polymoniales mainly on the basis of which characters?","image":"","options":["Root characters","Leaf venation","Floral characters","Stem anatomy"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"According to NCERT, Convolvulaceae and Solanaceae are placed in the order Polymoniales mainly on the basis of floral characters.\nRoots, leaves and stems are vegetative parts. They are not the main basis given for grouping these two families into this order.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-128","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":128,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (specific epithet) with List-II (organism):\nList-I\n(a) tuberosum\n(b) melongena\n(c) tigris\n(d) pardus\nList-II\n(i) Leopard\n(ii) Tiger\n(iii) Brinjal\n(iv) Potato","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) tuberosum: Solanum tuberosum is potato, so (iv).\n(b) melongena: Solanum melongena is brinjal, so (iii).\n(c) tigris: Panthera tigris is tiger, so (ii).\n(d) pardus: Panthera pardus is leopard, so (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-129","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":129,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In a biological name, the specific epithet is written beginning with a capital letter to distinguish it from the genus.\nStatement II: The name of the author is written in an abbreviated form after the specific epithet.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect: only the genus starts with a capital letter. The specific epithet starts with a small letter, as in Mangifera indica.\nStatement II is correct: the author's name comes at the end, after the epithet, in abbreviated form, e.g. Mangifera indica Linn.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-130","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":130,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Lion, tiger and leopard are placed in the same genus, Panthera.\nReason (R): A genus is an aggregate of closely related species that share more characters with each other than with species of other genera.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: lion (P. leo), tiger (P. tigris) and leopard (P. pardus) are all species of the genus Panthera.\nR is true: NCERT describes genera as aggregates of closely related species with more characters in common than species of other genera.\nR explains A: these three cats share several common features, which is why they are grouped in one genus.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-131","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":131,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Ernst Mayr pioneered the currently accepted definition of a biological species.\n(ii) Ernst Mayr has been called 'The Darwin of the 20th century'.\n(iii) The technical question hidden within 'what is life?' seeks the purpose of life.\n(iv) The number of described species is now fixed, because exploring already studied areas yields no new organisms.\n(v) Mayr received the Balzan Prize, the International Prize for Biology and the Crafoord Prize, together regarded as the triple crown of biology.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: Mayr pioneered the accepted biological species definition.\n(ii) Correct: Mayr is called 'The Darwin of the 20th century'.\n(iii) Incorrect: the technical question asks what living is as opposed to non-living. The purpose of life is the philosophical question, which scientists do not attempt to answer.\n(iv) Incorrect: new organisms are continuously identified, even in old areas.\n(v) Correct: these three prizes form the 'triple crown' of biology.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-132","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":132,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"In the taxonomic hierarchy of plants, classes that show a few similar characters are grouped into a higher category called:","image":"","options":["Family","Order","Division","Genus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that in plants, classes with a few similar characters are assigned to a higher category called Division.\nDivision in plants corresponds to phylum in animals.\nFamily, order and genus are all lower than class, so they cannot be formed by grouping classes.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-133","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":133,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Which scientist introduced the system of naming organisms with two components, now used by biologists all over the world?","image":"","options":["Ernst Mayr","Carolus Linnaeus","Charles Darwin","Alexander Agassiz"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that binomial nomenclature, naming an organism with two components, was given by Carolus Linnaeus.\nLinnaeus also wrote Systema Naturae.\nErnst Mayr is the most tempting wrong option. He pioneered the accepted definition of a biological species, not the binomial naming system.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-134","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":134,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Nomenclature\n(b) Identification\n(c) Classification\n(d) Systematics\nList-II\n(i) Grouping into convenient categories based on easily observable characters\n(ii) Study of the kinds and diversity of organisms and the relationships among them\n(iii) Standardised naming so that an organism has the same name all over the world\n(iv) Describing an organism correctly so that we know which organism a name belongs to","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Nomenclature is standardising names worldwide, so it matches (iii).\n(b) Identification means describing an organism correctly so the name can be attached to it, so it matches (iv).\n(c) Classification groups organisms into convenient categories using easily observable characters, so it matches (i).\n(d) Systematics studies diversity and the relationships among organisms, so it matches (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-135","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":135,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"The term 'systematics' is derived from the Latin word 'systema'. What does 'systema' mean?","image":"","options":["Naming of organisms with two words","Systematic arrangement of organisms","Study of the life processes of organisms","Grouping of organisms by their uses"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The Latin word 'systema' means systematic arrangement of organisms.\nLinnaeus used this root in the title of his publication, Systema Naturae.\nNaming with two words is binomial nomenclature, which is a different idea.\nGrouping organisms by their uses describes the earliest classifications, not the meaning of 'systema'.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-136","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":136,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, the number of species that are known and described lies in which range?","image":"","options":["0.7–0.8 million","1.7–1.8 million","17–18 million","170–180 million"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that about 1.7–1.8 million species are known and described.\nThis figure reflects biodiversity, meaning the number and types of organisms on earth.\nThe other options shift the decimal place. They are either far too small or far too large for the number of described species.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-137","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":137,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"The taxonomic unit ‘Phylum’ in the classification of animals is equivalent to which hierarchical level in classification of plants","image":"","options":["Class","Order","Division","Family"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: in animals, classes sharing a few similar characters are grouped into a phylum (e.g., Chordata).\nIn plants, the equivalent rank is called a Division.\nClass, Order and Family are lower ranks that exist in both plants and animals, so they are not equivalents of phylum.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 1 (MCQ Q5)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-138","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":138,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Match the families in List-I with their orders in List-II.\nList-I\nA. Muscidae\nB. Anacardiaceae\nC. Poaceae\nD. Hominidae\nList-II\ni. Primata\nii. Poales\niii. Diptera\niv. Sapindales\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-iv, B-iii, C-ii, D-i","A-iii, B-iv, C-ii, D-i","A-iii, B-ii, C-iv, D-i","A-i, B-iv, C-ii, D-iii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A-iii: Muscidae (housefly) belongs to order Diptera.\nB-iv: Anacardiaceae (mango) belongs to order Sapindales.\nC-ii: Poaceae (wheat) belongs to order Poales.\nD-i: Hominidae (man) belongs to order Primata.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-139","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":139,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Mango and wheat are expected to share more common characters with each other than man and housefly share with each other.\nReason (R): Mango and wheat belong to the same division, whereas man and housefly belong to different phyla.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true. Mango and wheat are both in division Angiospermae. Man is in phylum Chordata and the housefly is in phylum Arthropoda, so they share only the kingdom.\nA is true. The lower the shared taxon, the more characters the organisms have in common.\nMango and wheat share a lower taxon than man and housefly do, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-140","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":140,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"A student handwrites the scientific name of tiger. Which version follows the universal rules of nomenclature?","image":"","options":["Panthera Tigris, with both words underlined by one continuous line","panthera tigris, with each word underlined separately","Panthera tigris, with each word underlined separately","Panthera tigris, with only the generic name underlined"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Rule 1: the generic name starts with a capital letter and the specific epithet starts with a small letter.\nRule 2: when handwritten, each of the two words is underlined separately to show its Latin origin.\nOption (1) wrongly capitalises the epithet. Option (2) wrongly uses a small letter for the genus. Option (4) leaves the epithet without underlining.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-141","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":141,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Plant families are characterised on the basis of vegetative features alone.\nStatement II: The families Convolvulaceae and Solanaceae are included in the order Polymoniales mainly on the basis of floral characters.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I: Incorrect. NCERT states that families are characterised on the basis of both vegetative and reproductive features of plant species.\nStatement II: Correct. Convolvulaceae and Solanaceae are placed in the order Polymoniales mainly on the basis of floral characters.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-142","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":142,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Insects are treated as a taxonomic category and given a rank in classification.\nReason (R): Insects share common features such as three pairs of jointed legs, which makes them recognisable, concrete objects that can be classified.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT uses insects as its example of a group that is given a rank, or category.\nR is true. Insects share features such as three pairs of jointed legs.\nR explains A. Because of these shared features, insects are recognisable concrete objects that can be classified, so they are given a rank.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-143","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":143,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements based on NCERT Table 1.1 is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Musca is the genus of the housefly, and its family Muscidae is placed in order Diptera","Triticum aestivum belongs to order Poales and class Monocotyledonae","Family Hominidae is placed in order Primata, which is included in class Mammalia","Anacardiaceae and Poaceae are placed in the same order because both belong to Angiospermae"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the housefly's genus is Musca, its family is Muscidae and its order is Diptera.\n(2) Correct: this matches Table 1.1 for wheat.\n(3) Correct: man's family Hominidae lies in order Primata, which lies in class Mammalia.\n(4) Incorrect: sharing a division does not mean sharing an order. Anacardiaceae is in Sapindales, while Poaceae is in Poales.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-144","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":144,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Mango and wheat belong to the same division but to different classes.\nStatement II: Man and housefly belong to the same kingdom but to different phyla.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Mango and wheat are both in division Angiospermae. Mango is in class Dicotyledonae and wheat is in class Monocotyledonae.\nStatement II is correct. Both are in Kingdom Animalia. Man is in phylum Chordata and housefly is in phylum Arthropoda.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-145","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":145,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Fishes, amphibians, reptiles, birds and mammals are grouped together in phylum Chordata. On which common features is this grouping based?","image":"","options":["Three pairs of jointed legs and a segmented body","External ears and body hair","Presence of a notochord and a dorsal hollow neural system","Presence of feathers and a beak"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT groups these classes into phylum Chordata because they all have a notochord and a dorsal hollow neural system.\nThree pairs of jointed legs is the feature NCERT uses for insects.\nExternal ears and body hair describe mammals only, so they cannot define the whole phylum.\nFeathers and a beak are features of birds alone.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-146","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":146,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about scientific naming are correct?\n(i) The agreed principles for naming plants are given in ICBN.\n(ii) Scientific names ensure that each organism has only one name.\n(iii) A scientific name already used for one known organism is not given to any other known organism.\n(iv) Local names of an organism remain the same throughout a country, so they vary only between countries.\n(v) A description of an organism should allow people in any part of the world to arrive at the same name.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: ICBN gives the principles for naming plants.\n(ii) Correct: scientific names give each organism only one name.\n(iii) Correct: scientific names ensure the same name has not been used for another known organism.\n(iv) Incorrect: NCERT says local names vary even within a country.\n(v) Correct: a description should lead people anywhere in the world to the same name.\nSo four statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-147","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":147,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Members of which of the following taxa are expected to share the greatest number of common characteristics?","image":"","options":["Animals","Mammals","Chordates","Dogs"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A dog is a mammal, mammals are chordates and chordates are animals. So 'dogs' is the lowest of these taxa.\nThe lower the taxon, the more characteristics its members share.\n'Mammals' is tempting, but it includes dogs along with many other kinds. Its members therefore share fewer characters.\n'Animals' is the broadest taxon, with the fewest shared characters.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-148","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":148,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"A zoologist discovers and correctly describes a new species of beetle. Which of the following correctly describes how it should be named?","image":"","options":["Named under ICBN, with both words starting with capital letters","Named under ICZN as a two-word Latinised name, with the genus capitalised, the specific epithet in small letters, and both words in italics (or separately underlined if handwritten)","Given the local name of the region as its scientific name, so that local people can recognise it","Given two different scientific names, one for each country in which it occurs"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A beetle is an animal, so it is named under ICZN, not ICBN. This rules out option (1), which also wrongly capitalises the epithet.\nAccording to NCERT, the name should be a Latinised binomial with the genus capitalised and the specific epithet in small letters.\nIt is printed in italics, or each word is underlined separately when handwritten.\nLocal names vary from place to place, so option (3) is wrong.\nOne organism gets only one name, so option (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-149","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":149,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Biological names are printed in italics.\nReason (R): Printing in italics indicates the Latin origin of biological names.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: a universal rule of nomenclature is that biological names are printed in italics.\nR is true: NCERT states that names are printed in italics to indicate their Latin origin.\nR therefore gives the reason for A, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-150","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":150,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"On what basis were the earliest classifications of organisms made by human beings?","image":"","options":["Evolutionary relationships among organisms","Structure of the cell and its organelles","Uses of organisms for food, clothing and shelter","Floral and reproductive characters"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In early days, humans needed sources for their basic needs of food, clothing and shelter.\nSo the earliest classifications grouped organisms by their 'uses' to humans.\nEvolutionary relationships are the concern of systematics, which developed much later.\nCell structure and floral characters belong to modern taxonomic studies.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-151","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":151,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the taxonomic hierarchy is incorrect?","image":"","options":["As we move from species to kingdom, the number of common characteristics among members decreases","The lower the taxon, the more characteristics its members share","The higher the category, the easier it is to determine its relationship to other taxa at the same level","Species is the lowest category for organisms of both the plant and animal kingdoms"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: common characters decrease from species to kingdom.\n(2) Correct: lower taxa share more characters.\n(3) Incorrect: NCERT says the higher the category, the greater the difficulty in determining its relationship to other taxa at the same level. This is why classification becomes more complex at higher levels.\n(4) Correct: species is the lowest category for all organisms.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-152","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":152,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements:\n(i) Solanum nigrum and Solanum melongena are different species that belong to one genus.\n(ii) Genus is a higher taxonomic category than species.\n(iii) In Homo sapiens, Homo is the specific epithet and sapiens is the genus.\n(iv) Closely related species should be distinguishable from one another by distinct morphological differences.\nWhich of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["(i), (ii) and (iii) only","(i), (ii) and (iv) only","(ii), (iii) and (iv) only","(i) and (iv) only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: nigrum and melongena are distinct specific epithets within the genus Solanum.\n(ii) Correct: a genus is an aggregate of species, so it ranks above species.\n(iii) Incorrect: Homo is the genus and sapiens is the species; the order has been reversed.\n(iv) Correct: NCERT states that a species should be distinguishable from closely related species on the basis of distinct morphological differences.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-153","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":153,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) Classification\n(B) Taxa\n(C) Taxonomy\n(D) Nomenclature\nList-II:\n(i) Standardising names so that an organism has the same name all over the world\n(ii) Grouping into convenient categories based on easily observable characters\n(iii) Classifying all living organisms into different taxa based on characteristics\n(iv) Convenient categories such as dogs, mammals, wheat\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-ii, B-iv, C-iii, D-i","A-iii, B-iv, C-ii, D-i","A-ii, B-i, C-iii, D-iv","A-iv, B-ii, C-i, D-iii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Classification means grouping into convenient categories by easily observable characters, so A matches (ii).\n(B) Taxa are the categories themselves, such as dogs, mammals and wheat, so B matches (iv).\n(C) NCERT calls classifying organisms into taxa based on characteristics taxonomy, so C matches (iii).\n(D) Nomenclature standardises naming worldwide, so D matches (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-154","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":154,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Homo sapiens is placed in the phylum Chordata.\nReason (R): Members of phylum Chordata are characterised by three pairs of jointed legs.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. In NCERT Table 1.1, man belongs to phylum Chordata.\nR is false. Chordates are grouped by the presence of a notochord and a dorsal hollow neural system.\nThree pairs of jointed legs is the feature NCERT uses for insects, such as the housefly in Insecta under Arthropoda.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-155","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":155,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Potato and brinjal are two different species. To which genus do both of them belong?","image":"","options":["Datura","Petunia","Solanum","Mangifera"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT gives potato and brinjal as two different species of the same genus, Solanum.\nPotato is Solanum tuberosum and brinjal is Solanum melongena.\nDatura and Petunia are separate genera. They share the family Solanaceae with Solanum, so they are tempting but wrong.\nMangifera is the genus of mango.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-156","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":156,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. Nomenclature\nB. Identification\nC. Classification\nD. Systematics\nList-II\ni. Standardising names so that an organism has the same name all over the world\nii. Describing an organism correctly so that we know which organism a name is attached to\niii. Grouping into convenient categories based on easily observable characters\niv. Study of the kinds and diversity of organisms and the relationships among them\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-ii, B-i, C-iii, D-iv","A-i, B-ii, C-iv, D-iii","A-ii, B-i, C-iv, D-iii","A-i, B-ii, C-iii, D-iv"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A-i: Nomenclature standardises naming worldwide.\nB-ii: Identification means describing the organism correctly, so we know which organism a name refers to.\nC-iii: Classification groups organisms into convenient categories using easily observable characters.\nD-iv: Systematics studies the diversity of organisms and the relationships among them.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-157","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":157,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Describing an organism correctly, so that we know which organism a particular name is attached to, is termed","image":"","options":["Nomenclature","Identification","Systematics","Binomial system"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"According to NCERT, naming is possible only when the organism is described correctly and we know which organism the name refers to. This step is called identification.\nNomenclature is the naming step itself, and it depends on identification. This makes option (1) the tempting distractor.\nSystematics deals with the relationships among organisms.\nThe binomial system is the two-word format used for naming.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-158","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":158,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"A gibbon and a dog are compared using the NCERT taxonomic hierarchy. What is the lowest (most specific) taxonomic category that both animals share?","image":"","options":["Order","Phylum","Class","Family"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The gibbon belongs to order Primata. The dog belongs to order Carnivora (family Canidae).\nSince their orders differ, they cannot share a family or an order.\nBoth orders are placed in class Mammalia, so class is the lowest shared category.\nThey also share phylum Chordata, but phylum is a higher rank than class.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-159","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":159,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following groups are cited in NCERT as classes included in phylum Chordata?\nFishes, Amphibians, Insects, Reptiles, Birds, Mammals","image":"","options":["4","6","5","3"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT lists fishes, amphibians, reptiles, birds and mammals as classes that together form phylum Chordata.\nInsects (class Insecta) are not chordates. They belong to phylum Arthropoda, as shown for the housefly in Table 1.1.\nSo 5 of the 6 groups are chordate classes.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-160","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":160,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (taxon) with List-II (taxonomic category):\nList-I\n(a) Panthera\n(b) Felidae\n(c) Carnivora\n(d) Mammalia\nList-II\n(i) Class\n(ii) Order\n(iii) Family\n(iv) Genus\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Panthera is the genus of lion, tiger and leopard, so it matches (iv).\n(b) Felidae is the family that holds Panthera and Felis, so it matches (iii).\n(c) Carnivora is the order that includes Felidae and Canidae, so it matches (ii).\n(d) Mammalia is the class that includes the orders Carnivora and Primata, so it matches (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-161","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":161,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The lion (Panthera leo) and the domestic cat (Felis) are placed in the same family even though they belong to different genera.\nReason (R): A family is a group of related genera that share fewer similarities than the members of a genus or a species do.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT places the genus Panthera together with the genus Felis in the family Felidae.\nR is true. A family groups related genera that have fewer similarities than those found within a genus or a species.\nR explains A: Panthera and Felis are related genera, so they are grouped into one family.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-162","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":162,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Order Primata includes monkey, gorilla and gibbon.\n(ii) Order Carnivora, which includes tiger, cat and dog, is placed in class Mammalia.\n(iii) Class Mammalia consists of only two orders, Primata and Carnivora.\n(iv) Kingdom Animalia includes all animals belonging to various phyla.\n(v) Kingdom Plantae comprises all plants from various divisions.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: NCERT lists monkey, gorilla and gibbon under order Primata.\n(ii) Correct: Carnivora, with tiger, cat and dog, belongs to class Mammalia.\n(iii) Incorrect: NCERT notes that class Mammalia has other orders also.\n(iv) Correct: all animals from various phyla are assigned to Kingdom Animalia.\n(v) Correct: Kingdom Plantae comprises all plants from various divisions.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-163","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":163,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A lion and a dog share more common characters than a lion and a leopard do.\nStatement II: A lion and a dog belong to the same order, Carnivora, but to different families.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The lion and the leopard belong to the same genus, Panthera. The lion and the dog share only the order Carnivora, since they are in Felidae and Canidae respectively.\nThe lower the shared taxon, the more characters the organisms share. So the lion and leopard share more characters than the lion and dog. Statement I is false.\nStatement II is true.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-164","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":164,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Convenient categories such as 'Dogs', 'Cats', 'Wheat', 'Rice' and 'Plants', which we use to study organisms, are scientifically termed:","image":"","options":["Species","Populations","Taxa","Communities"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT calls these convenient categories taxa (singular: taxon).\n'Plants' is a taxon and so is 'Wheat', even though they are at very different levels.\n'Species' is only one particular level, so it cannot cover broad groups like 'Plants' or 'Animals'.\nPopulations and communities are ecological terms, not classification categories.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-165","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":165,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, approximately how many species are currently known and described?","image":"","options":["0.7 – 0.8 million","1.7 – 1.8 million","17 – 18 million","170 – 180 million"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the number of known and described species ranges between 1.7 and 1.8 million.\nThis number reflects biodiversity, the number and types of organisms present on earth.\nThe count is not final, because new organisms are still being identified in both new and old areas.\nOptions such as 17–18 million confuse described species with larger estimates of total species. NCERT gives only the described figure.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-166","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":166,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Genus represents","image":"","options":["An individual plant or animal","A collection of plants or animals","A group of closely related species of plants or animals","None of these"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: a genus is a group of related species. These species share more characters with each other than with species of other genera.\nExample: potato (Solanum tuberosum) and brinjal (S. melongena) both belong to the genus Solanum.\nOption (2) is too vague, because any random collection is not a genus.\nOption (1) describes an individual, not a taxonomic category.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 1 (MCQ Q4)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-167","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":167,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"In the scientific name of potato, $\\textit{Solanum tuberosum}$, the word Solanum represents the:","image":"","options":["Specific epithet","Family","Genus","Author who described it"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"By the rules of nomenclature, the first word of a biological name is the genus. The second word is the specific epithet.\nSo Solanum is the genus and tuberosum is the specific epithet.\nThe family of potato is Solanaceae, which is a different word. The author citation would appear after the epithet.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-168","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":168,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following genera are placed in the family Solanaceae according to NCERT?\nSolanum, Petunia, Felis, Datura, Mangifera, Musca","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT names three genera of Solanaceae: Solanum, Petunia and Datura.\nThe other three belong to different families:\n- Felis is in Felidae.\n- Mangifera is in Anacardiaceae.\n- Musca is in Muscidae.\nSo the count is 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-169","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":169,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, three genera are placed together in the plant family Solanaceae. Which of the following genera is NOT one of them?","image":"","options":["Solanum","Petunia","Datura","Mangifera"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT gives Solanum, Petunia and Datura as three different genera placed in the family Solanaceae.\nMangifera (mango) belongs to the family Anacardiaceae, so it is not in Solanaceae.\nSolanum is a tempting distractor because it was the example used for the genus. It is still one of the genera of Solanaceae.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-170","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":170,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following taxon–category pairs are correctly matched?\nMammalia – Class\nCarnivora – Order\nAngiospermae – Division\nPoaceae – Family\nDiptera – Class\nInsecta – Order","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Mammalia – Class: correct.\nCarnivora – Order: correct.\nAngiospermae – Division: correct (it is the division of mango and wheat).\nPoaceae – Family: correct.\nDiptera – Class: wrong, because Diptera is an order.\nInsecta – Order: wrong, because Insecta is a class.\nSo 4 pairs are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-171","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":171,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following pairs of organisms belong to the same genus?","image":"","options":["Lion and domestic cat","Potato and brinjal","Mango and potato","Tiger and dog"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Potato is Solanum tuberosum and brinjal is Solanum melongena, so both are species of the genus Solanum.\nThe lion belongs to Panthera, while cats belong to the separate genus Felis.\nMango (Mangifera) and potato (Solanum) are in different genera.\nThe tiger and the dog are not even in the same family; they belong to Felidae and Canidae.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-172","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":172,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In printed text, both words of a scientific name are written in italics.\nReason (R): Italics are used to indicate the Latin origin of biological names.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT says both words of a biological name are printed in italics.\nR is true: italics (or separate underlining when handwritten) are used to indicate the Latin origin of the name.\nR states the exact purpose of the italics, so it correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-173","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":173,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) ICBN\n(B) ICZN\n(C) Mangifera\n(D) Linn.\nList-II\n(i) Generic name of mango\n(ii) Code for naming animals\n(iii) Author citation\n(iv) Code for naming plants","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(A) ICBN is the International Code for Botanical Nomenclature, so it governs plants: (iv).\n(B) ICZN is the International Code of Zoological Nomenclature, so it governs animals: (ii).\n(C) Mangifera is the genus of mango: (i).\n(D) Linn. is the abbreviated author name: (iii).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-174","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":174,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Assume each name below is printed in italics. How many of the following are written correctly according to the universal rules of nomenclature?\nMangifera indica Linn.\nHomo Sapiens\npanthera leo\nSolanum tuberosum\nMusca Domestica","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Mangifera indica Linn.: Correct. The genus is capitalised, the epithet is in small letters, and the author's name is abbreviated at the end.\nHomo Sapiens: Wrong. The specific epithet must start with a small letter.\npanthera leo: Wrong. The genus must start with a capital letter.\nSolanum tuberosum: Correct.\nMusca Domestica: Wrong. The epithet is capitalised.\nSo 2 names are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-175","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":175,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the lion (Panthera leo), leopard (P. pardus), tiger (P. tigris), domestic cat (genus Felis), potato (Solanum tuberosum) and brinjal (S. melongena). Which of the following inferences is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Potato and brinjal belong to the same genus and therefore also to the same family, Solanaceae","A lion shares more characters with a leopard than with a domestic cat","The tiger and the domestic cat belong to the same family but to different genera","Panthera and Felis differ at the family level and are therefore placed in different orders"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct. Both potato and brinjal are in Solanum, which belongs to Solanaceae.\n(2) Correct. Lion and leopard share a genus. Lion and cat share only a family, and lower taxa share more characters.\n(3) Correct. Tiger (Panthera) and cat (Felis) are different genera, both in Felidae.\n(4) Incorrect. Panthera and Felis are both in the family Felidae and the order Carnivora, so they do not differ at the family level.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-176","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":176,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"In the classification of plants, classes that share a few similar characters are assigned to a higher category. What is this category called?","image":"","options":["Order","Family","Phylum","Division"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"For animals, related classes are grouped into a phylum.\nFor plants, NCERT calls the equivalent category a Division.\nAn example from Table 1.1 is Angiospermae, the division of both mango and wheat.\n'Phylum' is the tempting option, but it is the term used for animals.\nOrder and family are lower categories than class.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-177","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":177,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): 'Animals', 'mammals' and 'dogs' are all taxa, but they represent taxa at different levels.\nReason (R): A dog is a mammal and mammals are animals.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Each of these convenient categories is a taxon, and NCERT notes that taxa can indicate categories at very different levels.\nR is true. A dog is a mammal, and mammals are animals.\nR explains A. Because one group is nested inside the next, the three taxa occupy different levels: dogs lie within mammals, which lie within animals.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-178","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":178,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"The process of standardising the naming of living organisms, so that a particular organism is known by the same name all over the world, is called","image":"","options":["Identification","Classification","Nomenclature","Characterisation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT defines nomenclature as standardising the naming of organisms so that each one is known by the same name everywhere.\nIdentification is the correct description of an organism, which comes before naming.\nClassification groups organisms into convenient categories.\nCharacterisation is the study of an organism's characters.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-179","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":179,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Systematics differs from simple classification because it takes into account evolutionary relationships between organisms.\nReason (R): The word systematics is derived from the Latin word 'systema', meaning systematic arrangement of organisms.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that systematics takes into account evolutionary relationships between organisms.\nR is true: 'systema' is Latin for systematic arrangement. Linnaeus used it in the title Systema Naturae.\nR only explains where the word comes from. It does not explain why systematics considers evolutionary relationships.\nSo R is not the correct explanation of A.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-180","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":180,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following does NCERT describe as essential bases of modern taxonomic studies?\nExternal structure, internal structure, structure of cell, development process, ecological information, local names, usefulness to humans","image":"","options":["3","4","5","7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT lists five essential bases of modern taxonomy: external structure, internal structure, cell structure, development process and ecological information.\nLocal names are not a basis. They vary from place to place, which is exactly why standard names are needed.\nUsefulness to humans was the basis of the earliest classifications, not modern taxonomy.\nSo the count is 5.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-181","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":181,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Nomenclature is possible only after an organism has been correctly described and identified","Taxonomic studies help us know our bio-resources and are useful in agriculture, forestry and industry","The scope of systematics was initially broad, but it was later narrowed to deal only with naming organisms","Characterisation, identification, classification and nomenclature are processes basic to taxonomy"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: naming is possible only when we know which organism the name is attached to, that is, after identification.\n(2) Correct: taxonomy is useful in agriculture, forestry and industry, and for knowing our bio-resources.\n(3) Incorrect: the scope of systematics was enlarged, not narrowed. It began as the study of relationships and later came to include identification, nomenclature and classification.\n(4) Correct: these four processes are basic to taxonomy.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-182","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":182,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following ‘suffixes’ used for units of classification in plants indicates a taxonomic category of ‘family’?","image":"","options":["– Ales","– Onae","– Aceae","– Ae"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: in plant nomenclature, a family name ends in '-aceae'. Examples are Solanaceae, Poaceae and Malvaceae.\n'-ales' is the tempting distractor, but it marks an order, as in Polymoniales and Poales.\n'-ae' and '-onae' are not family endings.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 1 (MCQ Q2)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-183","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":183,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"In the scientific name of mango, Mangifera indica, the word 'indica' represents the:","image":"","options":["Genus","Family","Specific epithet","Author citation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In a binomial name, the first word is the genus and the second word is the specific epithet.\nSo Mangifera is the genus and indica is the specific epithet.\nMango's family is Anacardiaceae, which is not part of the binomial.\nThe author citation (Linn.) is a separate abbreviation written after the epithet.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-184","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":184,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are stated in NCERT as essential parts of the basis of modern taxonomic studies?\nExternal structure\nInternal structure\nStructure of the cell\nDevelopment process\nEcological information\nLocal names of the organism\nEconomic uses of the organism","image":"","options":["3","4","5","7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Modern taxonomy is based on external structure, internal structure, cell structure, development process and ecological information. These are 5 items.\nLocal names are not included. They vary from place to place, which is the reason standard scientific names are needed.\nEconomic uses were the basis of the earliest classifications, not modern taxonomy.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-185","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":185,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"As we go from species to kingdom in a taxonomic hierarchy, the number of common characteristics","image":"","options":["Will decrease","Will increase","Remain same","May increase or decrease"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Key fact: going up the hierarchy (species → genus → family → order → class → phylum/division → kingdom), each rank includes more, and more varied, organisms.\nA larger and more diverse group shares fewer features among all its members.\nSo the number of common characteristics decreases from species to kingdom.\n'Increase' fails because it reverses this trend. Species members share the most characters, and kingdom members share the fewest.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 1 (MCQ Q1)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-186","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":186,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Among the organisms listed below, which pair belongs to the same phylum or division?","image":"","options":["Man and housefly","Mango and wheat","Housefly and wheat","Man and mango"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Mango and wheat are both placed in the division Angiospermae, so they share a division.\nMan is in Chordata and the housefly is in Arthropoda, so they belong to different phyla.\nThe other two pairs mix an animal with a plant, so they are not even in the same kingdom.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-187","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":187,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Mango and wheat belong to the same division but to different classes.\nStatement II: The lower the taxon, the fewer the characteristics shared by its members.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Mango and wheat are both in division Angiospermae. Mango is in class Dicotyledonae and wheat in class Monocotyledonae.\nStatement II is incorrect. NCERT says the opposite: the lower the taxon, the more characteristics its members share.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-188","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":188,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Four students handwrote the scientific name of potato in their notebooks as described below. Which student followed all the rules of nomenclature?","image":"","options":["Student P: 'Solanum tuberosum' with one continuous line drawn under both words together","Student Q: 'Solanum tuberosum' with each word underlined separately","Student R: 'solanum Tuberosum' with each word underlined separately","Student S: 'Solanum Tuberosum' with each word underlined separately"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"When a name is handwritten, both words must be underlined separately.\nThe genus must begin with a capital letter and the specific epithet with a small letter.\nP: wrong, because one continuous line is used instead of separate underlining.\nR: wrong, because the capitalisation is reversed.\nS: wrong, because the epithet begins with a capital letter.\nQ meets every rule.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-189","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":189,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (organism) with List-II (order to which it belongs, as per NCERT Table 1.1).\nList-I:\n(A) Man\n(B) Housefly\n(C) Mango\n(D) Wheat\nList-II:\n(I) Poales\n(II) Sapindales\n(III) Diptera\n(IV) Primata","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Man is in order Primata, so A-IV.\n(B) Housefly is in order Diptera, so B-III.\n(C) Mango is in order Sapindales, so C-II.\n(D) Wheat is in order Poales, so D-I.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-LIVING-WORLD-190","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · The Living World","qno":190,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"The Living World","type":"mcq","question":"Which international code provides the principles and criteria for giving scientific names to animals?","image":"","options":["International Code for Botanical Nomenclature (ICBN)","International Code of Zoological Nomenclature (ICZN)","International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN)","International Code of Virus Classification and Nomenclature (ICVCN)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Animal taxonomists have developed the International Code of Zoological Nomenclature (ICZN) to name animals.\nICBN is the tempting wrong option. It applies to plants, not animals.\nIUCN is a conservation body, not a naming code.\nThe virus code is not the code NCERT gives for naming animals.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 1 · The Living World","difficulty":"easy"}]});
